lines=['# Multi-worker drain CPU and Spot latency diagnosis — #1639','',
'**Result.** The final software-only CPU profiles reproduce approximately 19 extra application µs/msg in both cells. Send spends about half of its increase in mutex/futex CPU; request spends about two fifths there and more in dispatch/reply preparation. The 7 ms Spot tail includes milliseconds of runnable CPU wait before handler execution and during completion. The measured PM call uses same-host **Instance Spot command 39**, rather than the ordinary `enqueue_local_spot_request` route assumed in the previous reports. No runtime fix or design recommendation is made.','',
'**Inputs and measurement boundary.** Main is frozen `3f83745bed140d2e50df747f5e157fb2ee02293e`; branch is `perf/1639-cpp-multi-drain`, `5a585edc93` plus the supplied ten uncommitted files. `original-state.json`, `inputs.json` and `preservation.json` identify the inputs and verify the target worktree. Production CPU profiles use the existing, unmodified cdrain/main-build and cdrain2/build executables; exact executable copies are retained in this job. Only diagnostic PM executables use copied source under `cdiag/main/` and `cdiag/branch/`.','',
'Condition B and the cdrain2 runner are retained: source 0–7, entire target 8–15, 1,024-byte payload, request window 100, send concurrency 8, 30 s warm-up. CPU profile cells use 12 s active time and approximately 10 s counter observations to obtain more samples at 99 Hz. PM keeps the original 30 s active time, approximately 25 s observation and 100 ms independent request cadence. Every collection holds `flock -o /root/jobs/fx/cx/measure.lock`. Builds hold the shared lock and invoke `cpp-build-slot.sh`; the installed wrapper accepts a command, so `build-instrumented.py` and `build-activation.py` limit compilation to three concurrent commands instead of passing an unsupported leading `--parallel` argument.','',
'**1. Measured application CPU attribution**','',
'The final collector uses `perf record -k mono -e cpu-clock -F 99 -a -C 8-15 --call-graph dwarf,16384`. Offline analysis filters the target PID and first-handler role-marked application TIDs. `/proc/.../schedstat` supplies application CPU and the matching target receive-count delta supplies the denominator. A group’s estimate is its non-overlapping sampled-period share multiplied by that observed application CPU. Samples are restricted to the counter window; perf startup means the sampled subwindow can be shorter. A mutex/futex row contains on-CPU lock, sleep-entry, scheduler and wake work, **not time spent asleep**. Call-chain classifications, sample counts and unscaled sample-period totals are retained in `attribution-perf-cpu.json`.','',
'| Cell | Main application µs/msg | Branch application µs/msg | Increase | Main / branch msg/s | Throughput change | Main / branch receive µs/msg | Main / branch app samples |','|---|---:|---:|---:|---|---:|---|---|']
